    21	row-specific cancellation lemma is proved, but freeness does not imply
    22	global monogenicity and the row-specific monogenicity question remains open.
    23	Thus the result below is a conditional monogenic-resolvent no-go, not a
    24	uniform kill of the row.
    25	
    26	## 1. What global monogenicity actually asks
    27	
    28	Put `R=C[x,y]` and let `B=pi_*O_X` be the finite-flat cubic algebra proved in
    29	the charged r2 report.  Since `3` is invertible,
    30	`B=R direct-sum E`, where `E=ker(Tr)`; Quillen--Suslin makes `E` free of
    31	rank two.  Choose a trace-zero basis `z,w`.  In Miranda's notation the four
    32	multiplication coefficients give the binary cubic (the index form)
    33	
    34	```text
    35	I(S,T)=B_0 S^3-3A_0 S^2T+3D_0 ST^2-C_0 T^3.          (1.1)
    36	```
    37	
    38	For `theta=sz+tw`, direct expansion in Miranda's multiplication table gives
    39	
    40	```text
    41	det_R(1,theta,theta^2)=I(s,t).                         (1.2)
    42	```
    43	
    44	Any generator can first be translated by one third of its trace; this does
    45	not change the generated algebra and puts it in `E`.
    46	
    47	Consequently the exact criterion is
    48	
    49	```text
    50	B is generated by one element over R
    51	  iff I(s,t) is in R^*=C^* for some s,t in R.          (1.3)
    52	```
    53	
    54	Indeed, a unit in (1.2) says that `1,theta,theta^2` is an `R`-basis.  It also
    55	forces `(s,t)` to be a unimodular column, so it extends to a matrix in
    56	`GL_2(R)`; in that basis the corresponding coefficient of `S^3` is a unit.
    57	Conversely a unit `S^3` coefficient gives a generator.  Translating a
    58	generator by one third of its trace then gives a monic depressed equation
    59	`Z^3+aZ+b`; its polynomial discriminant is the algebra discriminant (up to a
    60	constant square), hence
    61	
    62	```text
    63	4a^3+27b^2=kappa F,  kappa in C^*.                    (1.4)
    64	```
    65	
    66	This corrects the proposed “global linear factor” criterion.  A unimodular
    67	linear factor of `I` cuts out a section *inside* the cubic divisor of bad
    68	generators in `P(E)`; (1.3) asks for a section avoiding that divisor and
    69	taking a unit value.  These are different conditions, and factorization
    70	cannot replace (1.3).  In fact, if the generic algebra is a cubic
    71	field, `I` has no linear factor over `Frac(R)`: such a factor gives a nonzero
    72	trace-zero nongenerator, whereas every nonzero trace-zero element of a
    73	degree-three field generates it.  The algebra may nevertheless be
    74	monogenic.
    75	
    76	The discriminant and Hessian do not repair this.  They are covariants of the
    77	`GL_2(R)` action, not a classification of its integral orbits, and neither
    78	solves the nonlinear equation `I(s,t) in C^*`.  Freeness of `E` merely makes
    79	that equation global.  Thus the correct Miranda residual is a unit-
    80	representation problem, not a projective-module or factorization problem.
    81	
    82	Primary source: Miranda, *Triple Covers in Algebraic Geometry*, Theorem
    83	2.7.1 and Remark 2.8.1 (multiplication and a selected generator), Proposition
    84	3.3 and display (3.4) (binary cubic), Theorem 3.6/Definition 3.7 (global
