   112	`16(1-x)^3`, not a square.  Substitution `Y=y^2` cannot introduce a
   113	factor: comparison of two quadratic factors of an even quartic says that
   114	either this quadratic already splits or `(4x-3)/x^2` is a square in
   115	`C(x)`; neither is true.  Thus `G` is a reduced irreducible sextic.
   116	
   117	The algebra is connected and generically `S_3`.  Indeed Miranda's selected
   118	generator has polynomial
   119	`Z^3+3(x-1)Z+(3x-2+x^2y^2)`.  It is irreducible: over `C(x,Z)`, viewing it
   120	as a quadratic in `y` would require the negative of
   121	`Z^3+3(x-1)Z+3x-2` to be a square, impossible by its odd `Z`-degree.
   122	It is therefore irreducible in `C(x)[y,Z]`; primitivity in `Z` and Gauss's
   123	lemma give irreducibility in `C(x,y)[Z]`.
   124	The selected polynomial has discriminant `-27x^2G`, whose square class is
   125	`G`, hence is not a square.  Since the free algebra injects into its generic
   126	fiber, that generic field also makes it a domain.  It is normal: the algebra is
   127	free over the regular ring `R`, hence `S_2`; at a height-one prime away
   128	from `G` it is etale, while at the prime `(G)` its discriminant valuation
   129	is one.  The index-square formula over that DVR makes the order maximal,
   130	so it is `R_1`.
