    35	## 1. From the `S_4` quotient to a connected simply branched triple cover
    36	
    37	Let
    38	`V={1,(12)(34),(13)(24),(14)(23)}`.  Conjugation on the three nonidentity
    39	elements of `V` gives the quotient `q:S_4 -> S_3`, with kernel `V`.  A
    40	transposition is sent to a transposition.  If `tau,sigma` are disjoint
    41	transpositions, then `tau sigma` is a nonidentity element of `V`, and hence
    42	
    43	```text
    44	q(tau)=q(sigma).                                      (1.1)
    45	```
    46	
    47	Thus `psi=q o phi` is onto `S_3`; in its natural action on three letters it
    48	defines a connected degree-three etale cover of `U=A^2-D`.  Surjectivity, not
    49	just transitivity, says that the generic cubic extension has `S_3` Galois
    50	closure.
    51	
    52	By algebraic Riemann existence and normalization, this cover extends uniquely
    53	to a finite normal map `pi:X -> A^2`.  The local models also prove flatness.
    54	At a smooth point of `D`, with local equation `u=0`, the completed algebra is
    55	
    56	```text
    57	C[[u,v]]  direct-sum  C[[u,v]][t]/(t^2-u),             (1.2)
    58	```
    59	
    60	up to multiplying `u` by a unit.  At a node, choose `D:{uv=0}`.  Its two
    61	meridians both act by the same transposition by (1.1), while the third sheet is
    62	fixed, so the completed algebra is
    63	
    64	```text
    65	C[[u,v]]  direct-sum  C[[u,v]][t]/(t^2-uv).            (1.3)
    66	```
    67	
    68	The second factor in (1.3) is the normal `A_1` surface.  Both displayed
    69	algebras are free of rank three over the completed base.  Off `D` the map is
    70	etale; hence completion and faithful flat descent show that `pi` is finite
    71	flat everywhere.  Notice that `X` is allowed to be singular over a node.
    72	
    73	The algebra discriminants of (1.2) and (1.3) are respectively a unit times
    74	`u` and a unit times `uv`.  Therefore the divisorial discriminant has no
    75	component away from `D`, and every smooth branch of `D`, including both
    76	branches at each node, occurs with multiplicity exactly one.  If `f` is the
    77	reduced irreducible sextic defining `D`, factoriality of `C[x,y]` gives
    78	
    79	```text
    80	disc(pi_*O_X/C[x,y]) = kappa f,       kappa in C^*.     (1.4)
    81	```
    82	
    83	This establishes the connected, simply-branched resolvent cover and the exact
    84	reduced branch divisor.  It does not yet choose a global algebra generator.
    85	
    86	## 2. Global cubic form and the exact discriminant divisor
    87	
    88	Put `R=C[x,y]` and `B=pi_*O_X`.  Division by three splits the trace:
    89	`B=R direct-sum E`, where `E=ker(Tr)` is projective of rank two.  Quillen--
    90	Suslin makes `E` free.  This is the point at which the charged reduction
    91	overreaches: a free cubic algebra need not have a power basis.
    92	
    93	The general structure theorem for a flat triple cover is Miranda's.  After a
    94	basis of `E`, it has four polynomial coefficients `A_0,B_0,C_0,D_0`, or
    95	equivalently a binary cubic index form
    96	
    97	```text
    98	I(S,T)=B_0 S^3-3A_0 S^2T+3D_0 ST^2-C_0 T^3.           (2.1)
    99	```
   100	
   101	Its branch polynomial (up to a nonzero numerical factor) is
   102	
   103	```text
   104	B_0^2 C_0^2-3A_0^2 D_0^2+4A_0^3 C_0+4B_0 D_0^3
   105	 -6A_0B_0C_0D_0.                                      (2.2)
   106	```
   107	
   108	Formula (1.4) says that (2.2), for the resolvent cover, is `kappa f`.
   109	It does **not** reduce (2.2) to `4a^3+27b^2`.
   110	
   111	Indeed, for `theta=s z+t w` in the trace-zero module,
   112	`det(1,theta,theta^2)=I(s,t)`.  Thus a global power basis exists exactly when
   113	there are `s,t in R` for which `I(s,t)` is a unit.  Freeness of `E` supplies
   114	the variables in this equation, not a solution.  As a concrete negative
   115	control, the free rank-three domain
   116	
   117	```text
   118	B=R direct-sum Rz direct-sum Rw,
   119	z^2=xw,   zw=x(1-xy),   w^2=(1-xy)z
   120	```
   121	
   122	embeds in `C(x,y)[t]/(t^3-x^2(1-xy))` by `z=t`, `w=t^2/x`; the valuation at
   123	`x=0` proves irreducibility.  Its index form is
   124	`I=xS^3+(xy-1)T^3`.  The two coefficients generate the unit ideal, so this
   125	algebra is Zariski-locally monogenic.  Were `I(s,t)=c in C^*` globally,
   126	reduction modulo `xy-1` would give `x s^3=c` in `C[x,x^(-1)]`, impossible by
   127	the `x`-valuation modulo three.  This example is not claimed to have the row's
   128	reduced branch; it disproves “free and locally monogenic, therefore one
   129	global cubic” and isolates the missing strengthened lemma.
   130	
   131	If such a unit-representing element were proved to exist, translating it by
   132	one third of its trace would give
   133	
   134	```text
   135	B = R[z]/(z^3+a z+b),
   136	-(4a^3+27b^2)=kappa f.                                 (2.3)
   137	```
   138	
   139	The multiplicity-one assertion in (2.3) is valid by §1.  The further bounds
   140	`deg a<=2`, `deg b<=3` are a second missing step.  They follow immediately
   141	only when the top terms of `4a^3` and `27b^2` do not cancel.  Shioda's exact
   142	order-five Davenport--Stothers example is
   143	
   144	```text
   145	P=x(x^9+12x^6+60x^3+96),
   146	Q=x^15+18x^12+144x^9+576x^6+1080x^3+432,
   147	P^3-Q^2=-1728(3x^6+28x^3+108).
   148	```
   149	
   150	Taking `a=-3P`, `b=2Q` gives `deg(a,b)=(10,15)` but
   151	`4a^3+27b^2=186624(3x^6+28x^3+108)`.  The last polynomial is squarefree.
   152	Moreover the cubic is irreducible: a rational root would be polynomial; the
   153	resultant-square factor in its squarefree discriminant makes `3r^2+a`
   154	constant, and substitution would make the discriminant have degree ten, not
   155	six.  Its
   156	generic Galois group is therefore `S_3`.  Its branch is nevertheless a union
   157	of vertical lines, not the irreducible one-place row.  Thus excluding
   158	high-degree cancellation needs a new argument using exactly those stronger
   159	hypotheses.
   160	
   161	Accordingly the unconditional route stops at the four-coefficient identity
   162	(2.2).  The two precise missing statements are
   163	`OPEN[PI1S4-(6,4)-TRIPLE-COVER-GLOBAL-MONOGENICITY]` and
   164	`OPEN[PI1S4-(6,4)-TRIPLE-COVER-HIGH-DEGREE-CANCELLATION]`.  Sections 3--6 solve the charged
   165	one-variable system **conditionally** on both statements.
   166	
   167	## 3. Classification of the leading-form identity
   168	
   169	Here the charged bounded-degree premise is assumed.  Let `A=a_2` and
   170	`B=b_3` be homogeneous of degrees two and three, and let `L` be a nonzero
   171	linear form:
   172	
   173	```text
   174	4A^3+27B^2=cL^6.                                       (3.1)
   175	```
